steps.body:
Under the Act, sexual harassment includes any one or more of these **unwelcome** acts or behaviour, directly or by implication:
- physical contact and advances;
- a demand or request for sexual favours;
- making sexually coloured remarks;
- showing pornography;
- any other unwelcome physical, verbal or non-verbal conduct of a sexual nature.

The Act also says these circumstances, when linked to sexual conduct, may amount to sexual harassment:
- an implied or explicit **promise of preferential treatment** in her employment;
- an implied or explicit **threat of detrimental treatment**;
- an implied or explicit **threat about her present or future employment status**;
- **interference with her work** or creating an **intimidating, offensive or hostile** work environment;
- **humiliating treatment** likely to affect her health or safety.

For the inquiry, the IC and LC have the same powers as a **civil court** under the Code of Civil Procedure in respect of:
- **summoning and enforcing attendance** of any person and examining them on oath;
- requiring **discovery and production of documents**;
- any other matter that may be prescribed.

Every employer must, among other things:
- provide a **safe working environment**, including from persons coming into contact at the workplace;
- **display** the penal consequences of sexual harassment and the order constituting the IC at a conspicuous place;
- hold **workshops and awareness programmes** regularly, and orientation for IC members;
- provide **facilities** for the IC to deal with complaints and conduct inquiries;
- help secure attendance of the respondent and witnesses;
- make available information the IC needs;
- **assist the woman** if she chooses to file a complaint under criminal law;
- treat sexual harassment as **misconduct** under service rules and act on it;
- monitor timely submission of IC reports.

During the inquiry, on her **written request**, the IC may recommend that the employer:
- **transfer** the aggrieved woman or the respondent to another workplace;
- grant her **leave up to 3 months** (in addition to her normal leave);
- **restrain the respondent** from reporting on her work performance or writing her confidential report (or assign that to another officer);
- grant other relief as prescribed (e.g. restraining the respondent from supervising her academic or work activity).

The IC recommends the employer to:
- take action as per **service rules** (or as prescribed — e.g. written apology, warning, reprimand, withholding promotion or increments, termination, counselling, community service);
- **deduct** from the respondent's salary or wages an amount it considers appropriate to be paid to the aggrieved woman (or to her legal heirs). If deduction is not possible (e.g. he has left), the respondent may be directed to pay; if he fails, the amount can be recovered as arrears of land revenue via the District Officer.

